Effect on window responsiveness
Factor	Load all records	Pagination using SwingWorker
Database waiting	Can take longer	Usually shorter per request
EDT workload	Large table update can take noticeable time	Smaller table update
Memory pressure	Higher	Lower
Window interaction	May become sluggish during population	More responsive in general
User experience	Must wait for all records	Can view records sooner
Scalability	Less suitable for larger datasets	More suitable for large datasets

Important distinction: SwingWorker prevents database loading from blocking the EDT, but it does not make a large table update free. Adding 100,000 rows to a table model on the EDT can still make the interface unresponsive. Pagination reduces both the background data workload and the amount of work required to update the table.
Final evaluation and selected approach
Recommended: Pagination with SwingWorker
For the Campus Event Registration Desktop Application, pagination using SQL LIMIT and OFFSET with SwingWorker is the better design.

It retrieves only the required records, reduces memory usage, reduces the amount of data transferred, and keeps the application responsive while database operations are running.

Although loading all records at once is simpler, it becomes inefficient when the number of registrations increases. Therefore, pagination is more suitable for a real campus registration system that may contain thousands or hundreds of thousands of records.
